 # Spring Bean Life Cycle

 1. Instantiation
 2. Populating Properties
 3. BeanNameAware  (setBeanName())
 4. BeanFactoryAware (setBeanFactory())
 5. Pre- initialization BeanPostProcessors
 6. Initializing Bean (afterPropertiesSet())
 7. Custom init method
 8. Post- initialization BeanPostProcessor
 9. Bean In-use
10. Destruction


## Instantiation

  This is the phase where Spring IoC container creates an instance of the bean.
  This can happen through constructor invocation or using a factory method.

### Populating Properties

   Once the bean is instantiated, the IoC container injects values into the bean's
   properties and dependencies.

### BeanNameAware

   The purpose of this interface is to provide the bean with it's assigned name within the Spring IoC container.

   public class MyBean implements BeanNameAware{
     private String beanName;

     @Override
     public void setBeanName(String name){
        this.beanName=name;
        System.out.println("Bean name is set to: " + beanName);
     }
   }

####Pre- Initialization BeanPostProcessor

   If there are any classes that implement the BeanPostProcessor interface,
   the postProcessBeforeInitialization method is invoked before the actual initialization of the bean.

   Developers can use this phase to perform custom initialization logic

   public class CustomBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
     @Override
     public object postProcessBeforeInitialization (Object bean, String beanName){
       //perform pre-initialization processing on the bean

       System.out.println("Before Initialization: " + beanName);

       //Return the modified or original bean instance
       return bean;
     }

     @Override
     public Object postProcessAfterInitialization (Object bean, String beanName){
       //Perform post-initialization processing on the bean

       System.out.println("After Initialization: " + beanName);

       // Return post-initialization processing on the bean
       return bean;

     }
   }

##### Initializing Bean

   After the properties are set, the init method (if configured) is called,
   or if the bran implements InitializingBean interface,
   the afterPropertiesSet method is invoked.

   public class MyBean implements initializingBean {
     private String message;

     //Setter method for the 'message' properties

     public void setMessage (String message){
     this.message = message;

     }
     //custom initialization logic using InitializingBean
     @Override
     public void afterPropertiesSet() throws Exception {
       System.out.println("Bean is being initialized. " + "Custom message: " + message);
       //Additional initialization logic can go here
     }
   }

   here, MyBean class implements the initializingBean interface, and the afterPropertiesSet method is overridden.


##### Initialization

   After the properties are set, the init method (if configured) is called,
   or if the bean implements the InitializingBean interface the afterPropertiesSet method is invoked.

   public class MyBean {
     private String message;

     //setter method for the 'message' property

     public void setMessage(String message){
     this.message = message ;
     }

    //custom initialization logic using init method

    public void customInitMethod() {
      System.out.println("Bean is being initialized" + "Custom message : " + message);

      //Additional initialization logic go here
    }
   }

###### Post-initialization BeanPostProcessors

   If there are any classes implementing the beanPostProcessor interface,
   the postProcessAfterInitialization method is invoked after after the bean is initialized.

   public class CustomBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
     @Override
     public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization (Object bean, String beanName){
       //Perform pre- initialization processing on the bean
       System.out.println ("After Initialization : " + beanName);
       return bean;

       //Return the modified or original bean instance
     }
   }

   Developer can use this phase to perform custom logic after the bean has been fully initialized.


###### In Use

   The bean is now ready for use by the application.
   Clients can obtain can object references to the bean and use it's functionality.

####### Destruction

   When the application context is shutting down, beans that implement
   the DisposableBean interface have their destroy method called.

    public class MyBean implements DisposableBean {
      //Custom cleanup logic using DisposableBean
      @Override
      public void destroy() throws Exception {
      System.out.println("Bean is being destroyed. ");

      //Additional cleanup logic can go here
      }
    }
